::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 6 + 2 &times; 5. {
~A. 40#This adds 6 + 2 first, then multiplies. Multiplication comes before addition here.
=B. 16#Correct. Multiply first: 2 &times; 5 = 10. Then 6 + 10 = 16.
~C. 13#This does not correctly apply multiplication before addition. Start with 2 &times; 5.
~D. 30#This multiplies 6 &times; 5 and ignores the structure of the expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (9 - 3)<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 12#This subtracts but does not square the result. The exponent still applies.
~B. 78#This squares 9 and subtracts 3. Grouping symbols must be simplified first.
=C. 36#Correct. First 9 - 3 = 6. Then 6<sup>2</sup> = 36.
~D. 6#This stops after evaluating inside the parentheses. The exponent must still be evaluated.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 20 - 4 &times; 3. {
~A. 48#This subtracts first, then multiplies. Multiplication must be completed before subtraction.
~B. 17#This subtracts 3 from 20 and does not use 4 &times; 3 correctly.
~C. 12#This stops after multiplication and does not complete the subtraction.
=D. 8#Correct. First 4 &times; 3 = 12. Then 20 - 12 = 8.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 5<sup>2</sup> + 6. {
=A. 31#Correct. 5<sup>2</sup> = 25, and 25 + 6 = 31.
~B. 11#This adds 5 and 6 without evaluating the exponent. Exponents come before addition.
~C. 55#This may treat the exponent as multiplication by 2 after adding. Evaluate 5<sup>2</sup> first.
~D. 36#This squares 6 instead of 5. The exponent applies only to 5.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 24 &divide; 6 + 7. {
~A. 2#This adds 6 + 7 first, then divides. Addition does not come before division here.
~B. 4#This stops after the division and does not add 7.
=C. 11#Correct. Divide first: 24 &divide; 6 = 4. Then 4 + 7 = 11.
~D. 17#This may divide incorrectly or add before simplifying. Complete 24 &divide; 6 first.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3(4 + 5). {
~A. 17#This adds 3 + 4 + 5 instead of multiplying by the grouped value.
=B. 27#Correct. First 4 + 5 = 9. Then 3 &times; 9 = 27.
~C. 23#This multiplies 3 &times; 4 and then adds 5. The grouping symbols must be simplified first.
~D. 12#This multiplies 3 &times; 4 and ignores the + 5 inside the parentheses.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 18 &divide; 3 &times; 2. {
=A. 12#Correct. Work from left to right: 18 &divide; 3 = 6, then 6 &times; 2 = 12.
~B. 3#This multiplies 3 &times; 2 first. Multiplication and division are completed from left to right.
~C. 6#This stops after 18 &divide; 3. The expression also includes &times; 2.
~D. 36#This multiplies 18 &times; 2 and ignores the division order.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 14 - 6 + 2. {
~A. 6#This subtracts the sum 6 + 2 from 14. Addition and subtraction are completed from left to right.
~B. 22#This adds all the numbers and ignores the subtraction sign.
~C. 12#This adds 6 + 2 after subtracting incorrectly. Work from left to right.
=D. 10#Correct. Work left to right: 14 - 6 = 8, and 8 + 2 = 10.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2<sup>3</sup> + 4 &times; 2. {
~A. 20#This may add 8 + 4 first, then multiply. Multiplication must be completed before addition.
~B. 14#This treats 2<sup>3</sup> as 6. Remember that 2<sup>3</sup> means 2 &times; 2 &times; 2.
=C. 16#Correct. 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 and 4 &times; 2 = 8, so 8 + 8 = 16.
~D. 24#This multiplies after adding terms. Follow exponent and multiplication before addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (8 + 2) &divide; 5. {
~A. 9.6#This divides 2 by 5 first. Grouping symbols must be simplified first.
=B. 2#Correct. First 8 + 2 = 10. Then 10 &divide; 5 = 2.
~C. 10#This stops after simplifying the grouping symbols. The division must still be completed.
~D. 15#This adds 8 + 2 + 5 instead of dividing by 5.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 4x + 1 when x = 3. {
~A. 8#This adds 4 + 3 + 1 instead of multiplying 4 by the value of x.
~B. 12#This stops after 4 &times; 3 and does not add 1.
~C. 16#This adds 4 to the product instead of adding 1. Check the expression carefully.
=D. 13#Correct. Substitute 3 for x: 4(3) + 1 = 12 + 1 = 13.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 6n - 2 when n = 5. {
=A. 28#Correct. Substitute 5 for n: 6(5) - 2 = 30 - 2 = 28.
~B. 24#This may subtract before multiplying. Substitute first, then multiply before subtracting.
~C. 30#This stops after 6 &times; 5 and does not subtract 2.
~D. 33#This adds instead of subtracting after substitution.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate a<sup>2</sup> + 3 when a = 4. {
~A. 11#This doubles 4 instead of squaring 4. The exponent means 4 &times; 4.
~B. 25#This adds first and then squares. There are no grouping symbols around a + 3.
=C. 19#Correct. Substitute 4 for a: 4<sup>2</sup> + 3 = 16 + 3 = 19.
~D. 16#This stops after evaluating the exponent and does not add 3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2(y + 6) when y = 3. {
~A. 12#This multiplies 2 &times; 6 and ignores the value substituted for y.
=B. 18#Correct. Substitute 3 for y: 2(3 + 6) = 2(9) = 18.
~C. 15#This adds 2 + 3 + 6 instead of multiplying by the grouped value.
~D. 36#This multiplies 2 &times; 3 &times; 6 instead of simplifying inside the grouping symbols.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 5m &divide; 2 when m = 6. {
=A. 15#Correct. Substitute 6 for m: 5(6) &divide; 2 = 30 &divide; 2 = 15.
~B. 13#This adds 5 + 6 + 2 instead of using multiplication and division.
~C. 60#This multiplies all three numbers. The expression says to divide by 2 after multiplying.
~D. 30#This stops after 5 &times; 6 and does not divide by 2.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3p + 2<sup>2</sup> when p = 4. {
~A. 20#This may add 3(4) and 2, but 2 must be squared.
=B. 16#Correct. Substitute 4 for p: 3(4) + 2<sup>2</sup> = 12 + 4 = 16.
~C. 24#This treats 2<sup>2</sup> as a multiplier or combines steps incorrectly.
~D. 14#This adds only 2 instead of evaluating 2<sup>2</sup>.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 30 - (4 + 2)<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 24#This subtracts 6 from 30 and does not square the grouped value.
~B. 28#This squares only 2 or does not apply the grouping symbols correctly.
~C. 34#This adds instead of subtracting after evaluating the grouped expression.
=D. -6#Correct. First 4 + 2 = 6. Then 6<sup>2</sup> = 36. Finally 30 - 36 = -6.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 7 + 18 &divide; 3. {
=A. 13#Correct. Divide first: 18 &divide; 3 = 6. Then 7 + 6 = 13.
~B. 25#This adds 7 + 18 first. Division must be completed before addition.
~C. 8.3#This appears to divide after adding. Follow the order of operations.
~D. 6#This stops after the division and does not add 7.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 4(10 - 7) + 5. {
~A. 7#This simplifies only 10 - 7 and adds 4 incorrectly. The 4 means multiplication.
~B. 12#This adds 10 - 7 + 5 and ignores multiplication by 4.
=C. 17#Correct. First 10 - 7 = 3. Then 4(3) + 5 = 12 + 5 = 17.
~D. 32#This multiplies 4 &times; 10 - 7 incorrectly. Grouping symbols must be simplified first.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 100 &divide; (2<sup>2</sup> + 6). {
~A. 56#This divides 100 by 2 first or combines operations out of order. Start inside the grouping symbols.
=B. 10#Correct. Inside the parentheses, 2<sup>2</sup> + 6 = 4 + 6 = 10. Then 100 &divide; 10 = 10.
~C. 25#This uses only 2<sup>2</sup> in the denominator and ignores + 6.
~D. 96#This subtracts the denominator from 100 instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says 5 + 3<sup>2</sup> = 8<sup>2</sup> = 64. Which correction is best? {
=A. 5 + 3<sup>2</sup> = 5 + 9 = 14#Correct. The exponent is evaluated before addition, so 3<sup>2</sup> becomes 9 first.
~B. 5 + 3<sup>2</sup> = 8#This ignores the exponent. The 3 must be squared before adding.
~C. 5 + 3<sup>2</sup> = 25 + 9 = 34#This squares both 5 and 3 even though only 3 has an exponent.
~D. 5 + 3<sup>2</sup> = 15<sup>2</sup> = 225#This multiplies before using the exponent and changes the expression structure.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Why is 4 + 6 &times; 2 equal to 16 instead of 20? {
~A. Addition always comes before multiplication.#This is the opposite of the order of operations. Multiplication comes before addition here.
~B. The expression should be read strictly from left to right for all operations.#Left-to-right applies within multiplication/division and within addition/subtraction, not across all operations.
~C. The 4 should be multiplied by 6 first.#There are no grouping symbols or multiplication sign connecting 4 and 6.
=D. Multiplication is completed before addition when there are no grouping symbols changing the order.#Correct. First 6 &times; 2 = 12, then 4 + 12 = 16.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression (6 + 4)<sup>2</sup> &divide; 5, what should be done first? {
~A. Square the 4#The exponent applies to the entire grouped value, not just the 4.
~B. Divide by 5#Division comes after grouping symbols and exponents in this expression.
=C. Add 6 + 4#Correct. Grouping symbols are simplified first.
~D. Square the 6#The exponent does not apply to 6 alone. First simplify the grouped expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2x<sup>2</sup> when x = 3. {
=A. 18#Correct. Substitute 3 for x: 2(3<sup>2</sup>) = 2(9) = 18.
~B. 12#This multiplies 2 &times; 3 &times; 2 instead of squaring x. The exponent applies to x.
~C. 36#This squares 2x, but the expression is 2 times x<sup>2</sup>.
~D. 8#This adds 2 and 3<sup>2</sup> incorrectly. The 2 is a multiplier.
}

::ALG1_U01_L04_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (2x)<sup>2</sup> when x = 3. {
~A. 18#This evaluates 2x<sup>2</sup>, but the grouping symbols mean 2x is squared as a group.
~B. 12#This doubles 3 and then doubles again instead of squaring the grouped value.
~C. 9#This squares only x and ignores the factor 2 inside the grouping symbols.
=D. 36#Correct. Substitute 3 for x: (2 &times; 3)<sup>2</sup> = 6<sup>2</sup> = 36.
}
